::ENG2_U06_L08_GP_Q01::[MLA.ENG2.C.01] Which statement best matches a presentation purpose of explaining a proposal to school leaders? {
=A. The school should provide a supervised study space after dismissal because many students remain on campus. #Correct. This states a clear proposal and reason for the intended audience.
~B. Study spaces are nice and school is a place. #Incorrect. This is vague and does not clearly explain the proposal.
~C. Everyone should agree with me because I said so. #Incorrect. This does not use respectful reasoning.
~D. My presentation is about several unrelated school topics. #Incorrect. This lacks a clear focus.
}

::ENG2_U06_L08_GP_Q02::[MLA.ENG2.C.02] Which slide choice best supports audience understanding? {
~A. A colorful background with tiny unlabeled numbers #Incorrect. The audience may not understand the evidence.
=B. A labeled chart showing 68 of 120 surveyed students stay after school #Correct. This makes the key evidence visible and understandable.
~C. A full paragraph copied from the speaker notes #Incorrect. Too much text weakens slide clarity.
~D. A decorative image with no evidence #Incorrect. Decoration does not support the claim.
}

::ENG2_U06_L08_GP_Q03::[MLA.ENG2.RS.01] Which evidence is most credible for the presentation? {
~A. Some students probably want a study space. #Incorrect. This is a guess, not evidence.
~B. I think the idea sounds useful. #Incorrect. This is an opinion without support.
=C. In a survey of 120 students, 68 reported staying on campus after school at least twice a week. #Correct. This is specific, measurable evidence.
~D. Everyone knows hallways are loud. #Incorrect. This is a broad claim without a source.
}

::ENG2_U06_L08_GP_Q04::[MLA.ENG2.V.03] Which sentence has the most appropriate tone for school leaders? {
~A. This plan is awesome and people should just do it. #Incorrect. This is casual and demanding.
~B. The school better make this happen. #Incorrect. This tone is not respectful or academic.
~C. Lots of kids hang around, so yeah. #Incorrect. This wording is casual and incomplete.
=D. The proposal would provide students with a supervised academic workspace after dismissal. #Correct. This is formal, precise, and audience-appropriate.
}

::ENG2_U06_L08_GP_Q05::[MLA.ENG2.C.03] What should a student check before completing the Unit 6 Assessment? {
~A. Whether every slide uses a different style #Incorrect. Inconsistent design can distract from the message.
=B. Whether the claim, evidence, slide, speaker note, tone, and conventions work together #Correct. Unit 6 synthesis requires all communication parts to align.
~C. Whether the speaker note repeats the slide exactly #Incorrect. The note should explain evidence, not simply repeat it.
~D. Whether the presentation avoids evidence #Incorrect. Evidence is necessary for a supported presentation.
}
